2026-10-30 04:54:01 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 20, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Then next week, he paints 1/4 the number he did this week. I need to find out how many ceilings are left to paint after next week. Hmm, let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, the initial number of ceilings is 28. This week, he paints 12. So the remaining ceilings after this week would be 28 minus 12. Let me calculate that. 28 minus 12... well, 28 minus 12 is 16. So after this week, there are 16 ceilings left. \n\nNow, the problem says next week he paints 1/4 the number he did this week. This week he painted 12, so 1/4 of that is 12 divided by 4. Let me do that division. 12 divided by 4 is 3. So next week, he paints 3 more ceilings. Therefore, the number of ceilings remaining after next week would be 16 (from before) minus 3 (painted next week), which is 13. \n\nWait, let me check again. The initial total is 28. Subtract 12 to get 16. Then subtract 3 to paint next week, giving 13. Yes, that seems right. \n\nBut wait, let me make sure there's no trick here. The problem says \"he paints 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week.\" So if this week he painted 12, next week he paints 1/4 of that. So 12 divided by 4 is 3, correct. \n\nSo after next week, he paints 3, so total left is 16 - 3 = 13. Therefore, the answer should be 13. \n\nI need to put the answer in a box and make sure. Let me confirm once more. 28 - 12 is 16. Then 16 minus 3 is 13. Yep. \n\nAnother way: 28 minus 12 is 16. 1/4 of 12 is 3. Therefore, 16 minus 3 is 13. Correct. \n\nSo the answer is 13. Therefore, put it in \\boxed{13}.\n</think>\n\nMichelangelo starts with 28 ceilings to paint this week. He paints 12 of them, leaving 28 - 12 = 16 ceilings uncolored.\n\nNext week, he paints 1/4 of what he painted this week, which is 12. Therefore, 12 divided by 4 equals 3. So he paints 3 more ceilings.\n\nAfter next week, the number of ceilings left to paint is the remaining 16 minus the 3 he paints this week.\n\n$$\n28 - 12 - 3 = 13\n$$\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{13}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?", "answer": "After this week, Michelangelo will have 28-12=<<28-12=16>>16 more ceilings to paint.\nNext week, Michelangelo will paint 12/4=<<12/4=3>>3 ceilings.\nMichelangelo will have 16-3=<<16-3=13>>13 more ceilings to paint.\n#### 13", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["13"], "solution": "13"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 2