,000 liters of air?\n\nNow, he has 500-liter tanks. Each tank is 500 liters. So how many of these would he need to buy to get 10,000 liters?\n\nWell, each tank is 500 liters, so if he buys one tank, he gets 500 liters. Thus, to get 10,000 liters, he would need 10,000 divided by 500 liters per tank. Let me do that division. 10,000 divided by 500 is 20. Therefore, he needs 20 tanks. \n\nWait, but let me check again. 500 liters per tank. 20 tanks would be 20*500 = 10,000 liters. Exactly. So that seems right. Therefore, the answer is 20.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I didn't miss any other factors. Is there anything else John has to consider? The problem says he buys 500-liter tanks, so he can buy the needed number of tanks, perhaps? But does he need to buy all the balloons at once? So maybe he needs to buy enough from the Tanks to supply all the balloons. Therefore, if he needs 10,000 liters, and each tank has exactly 500 liters, then 20 tanks are needed. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting some different approach? Let me think again. For example, if each balloon takes 10 liters, then 1000 balloons take 10,000 liters. Therefore, if he uses the 500-liter tanks, how many do he need? Since each tank is 500 liters, then 20*500 = 10,000. So yes, exactly. Therefore, the answer is 20.\n\nBut just to confirm, let me think about another way. Suppose he buys 500-liter tanks. Each full tank is 500 liters. If he fills all the balloons, which require 10,000 liters, then he needs 20 tanks. Yes.\n\nWait, could there be a mistake here? Maybe if some tanks were overfilled, or if the balloons can be filled in multiple ways? But the problem says he buys 500-liter tanks, which presumably are all full, so he needs to buy exactly the number of tanks as required. Therefore, 20 tanks. \n\nSo, putting it all together, the answer is 20, so \\boxed{20}\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{20}\n</think>\n\nJohn buys 1000 balloons, each containing 10 liters of liquid, to fill them up. We are informed that he also has a supply of 500-liter tanks.\n\n---\n\nTo determine how many tanks John needs to buy to fill all the balloons, first calculate the total liters of air needed.\n\nEach balloon requires 10 liters, and there are 1000 balloons:\n\n$$\n1000 \\text{ balloons} \\times 10 \\text{ liters per balloon} = 10,000 \\text{ liters}\n$$\n\nNow, each 500-liter tank contains exactly 500 liters. So to get 10,000 liters of gas, we need to divide the total by the amount per tank:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10,000 \\text{ liters}}{500 \\text{ liters per tank}} = 20 \\text{ tanks}\n$$\n\n---\n\nTherefore, the total number of 500-liter tanks John needs to buy is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{20}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?", "answer": "He needs 1000*10=<<1000*10=10000>>10000 liters of gas\nSo he needs 10000/500=<<10000/500=20>>20 tanks\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 220, 8886, 46756, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 220, 1416, 566, 49531, 220, 20, 15, 15, 85594, 25866, 315, 6819, 11, 1246, 1657, 25866, 1558], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 3757, 6801, 311, 3695, 69480, 323, 25866, 311, 5155, 1105, 705, 13, 6771, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 419, 700, 3019, 553, 3019, 13, 5512, 11, 1077, 752, 1349, 279, 3491, 1549, 311, 1281, 2704, 358, 3535, 678, 279, 3565, 382, 130