g: 14 + 2 = 16\n\nYes, that seems correct. So Greg is 16 years old. Let me see if there's any possibility I mixed up the years. For example, could it be 2 years older than Marcia, but 14 + 2 is indeed 16. No, that seems straightforward. So the answer should be boxed 16, which would be written in \\boxed{16}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine Greg's age, we start with the given information:\n\n- Cindy is 5 years old.\n- Jan, Cindy's sister, is 2 years older, so Jan is $ 5 + 2 = 7 $ years old.\n- Marcia, their oldest sister, is twice as old as Jan, so $ 2 \\times 7 = 14 $ years old.\n- Greg is two years older than Marcia, so $ 14 + 2 = 16 $ years old.\n\nThus, Greg's age is $\\boxed{16}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Cindy was 5 years old.  Her sister Jan was 2 years older than Cindy.  Their oldest sister Marcia was twice as old as Jan.  Greg was two years older than Marcia.  How old was Greg?", "answer": "Jan was 2 years older than Cindy who was 5 so 2+5 = <<2+5=7>>7 years old\nMarica was twice as old as Jan so 2*7 = <<2*7=14>>14 years old\nGreg was 2 years older than Marcia who was 14 so 2+14 = <<2+14=16>>16 years old\n#### 16", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Cindy was 5 years old.  Her sister Jan was 2 years older than Cindy.  Their oldest sister Marcia was twice as old as Jan.  Greg was two years older than Marcia.  How old was Greg?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["16"], "solution": "16"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 69331, 572, 220, 20, 1635, 2310, 13, 220, 6252, 12923, 4350, 572, 220, 17, 1635, 9014, 1091, 69331, 13, 220, 10964, 23513, 12923, 18880, 685, 572, 10917, 438, 2310, 438, 4350, 13, 220, 16046, 572, 1378, 1635], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1430, 311, 11625, 419, 3491, 3019, 553, 3019, 1