2026-10-30 05:10:31 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 32, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Rafael works 10 hours on Monday and 8 hours on Tuesday on his delivery job. With 20 hours left to work in the week, how much money does Rafael make if he is paid $20 per hour?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Rafael works 10 hours on Monday and 8 hours on Tuesday on his delivery job. With 20 hours left to work in the week, how much money does Rafael make if he is paid $20 per hour?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so Rafael works 10 hours on Monday and 8 hours on Tuesday. That adds up to 18 hours so far. The week has 20 hours left. Hmmm, need to find out how much money he makes if he's paid $20 per hour. Alright, let me think.\n\nFirst, I need to find out the total hours he has left. If he has 20 hours left, minus the 18 hours he's already worked, that would be 2 hours remaining. So he has 2 hours left.\n\nThen, if he's paid $20 per hour, then 2 hours would be 2 times $20. That makes $40. Let me check that again. 10 hours times $20 is $200, but that's if he worked all the hours. Wait, no, the problem says he's paid $20 per hour, so maybe it's 20 hours total? Wait, but Rafael is only working 20 hours left. Wait, maybe I need to calculate his total hours worked so far and then determine how much he's paid.\n\nWait, let me re-read the problem. \"Rafael works 10 hours on Monday and 8 hours on Tuesday on his delivery job. With 20 hours left in the week, how much money does Rafael make if he is paid $20 per hour?\" So, total work hours in the week is 24 hours. He has 20 left. So he has 4 hours left. Then total payment would be 4 hours times $20. Which is $80. Wait, that seems too low. Wait, maybe I misunderstood the problem.\n\nWait, if he is paid $20 per hour, then total money is hours times 20. So if he has 2 hours left, that's 2*20= $40. So answer is $40. But let me check again. Let's see, if he worked 10+8=18 hours so far, so remaining hours are 2. So 2*20=40. So the answer should be $\\boxed{40}$. But let me think once again. Maybe I made a mistake with the 24 hours total?\n\nWait, the problem says \"With 20 hours left in the week.\" So does that mean 20 hours remaining in the week, or does it mean he has 20 hours left in total? Well, the wording is \"20 hours left in the week.\" So maybe he's working 20 hours in total remaining. But he has 10+8=18 already worked, so total remaining is 2. So 2 hours, times $20, equals $40. So yes, answer is 40.\n\nHmm. So why does the problem say \"With 20 hours left in the week\"? Maybe that's a red herring? But that seems like an extra note. But regardless, I think the key is that he has 2 hours left, times $20 per hour. So the answer is 40.\n\nTherefore, boxed answer is \\boxed{40}.\n</think>\n\nRafael works 10 hours on Monday and 8 hours on Tuesday, totaling 18 hours. The total remaining hours in the week is:\n\n$$ 20 \\text{ hours on Monday} + 20 \\text{ hours on Tuesday} = 20 \\text{ hours left} $$\n\nHe is paid $20 per hour. Therefore, the total amount of money he makes for the remaining 2 hours is:\n\n$$ 2 \\text{ hours} \\times 20 \\text{ dollars/hour} = 40 \\text{ dollars} $$\n\n---\n\n$$\n\\boxed{40}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Rafael works 10 hours on Monday and 8 hours on Tuesday on his delivery job. With 20 hours left to work in the week, how much money does Rafael make if he is paid $20 per hour?", "answer": "The total number of hours that Rafael has worked by Tuesday is 8+10 = <<8+10=18>>18 hours.\nTo complete his assigned work hours, Rafael works 20 hours more, making his total weekly hours to be 18+20 = <<38=38>>38 hours.\nSince he is paid $20 per hour, he makes $20*38 = $<<20*38=760>>760 a week.\n#### 760", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Rafael works 10 hours on Monday and 8 hours on Tuesday on his delivery job. With 20 hours left to work in the week, how much money does Rafael make if he is paid $20 per hour?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["760"], "solution": "760"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 54400, 4278, 220, 16, 15, 4115, 389, 7014, 323, 220, 23, 4115, 389, 7589, 389, 806, 9691, 2618, 13, 3085, 220, 17, 15, 4115, 2115, 311, 975, 304, 279, 2003, 11, 1246, 1753, 3220, 1558, 54400, 1281], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 54400, 4278, 220, 16, 15, 4115, 389, 7014, 323, 220, 23, 4115, 389, 7589, 13, 2938, 11367, 705, 311, 220, 16, 23, 4115, 773, 3041, 13, 576, 2003, 702, 220, 17, 15, 4115, 2115, 13, 472, 48886, 11, 1184, 311, 1477, 700, 1246, 1753, 3220, 566, 3643, 421, 566, 594, 7171, 400, 17, 15, 817, 6460, 13, 97593, 11]}
