Therefore, Happy Good Healthy Family has 53 cows.\n\nSo, total cows taken to ranch would be We the People's 17 plus Happy Good Healthy Family's 53. So total is 17 + 53. Let me add those together. 17 + 50 is 67, and then +3 more is 70. So, 70 cows in total?\n\nWait, is that right? Let me check again. \n\nWe the People: 17\n\nHappy Good Healthy Family: 3*17 + 2 = 51 + 2 = 53. \n\nAdding 17 + 53: 17 + 53. 10 + 50 = 60, 7 + 3 = 10, so total 70. Yes, that's 70. So the answer is 70. But let me think again if there's a possibility I did something wrong. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I misread the problem. Let me check the problem again. The problem says: \"We the People has 17 cows. Happy Good Healthy Family has two more than three times the number of cows We the People has. If their cows are taken to a ranch to graze together, how many cows will be in the ranch altogether?\"\n\nSo, the key here is that they are taking their cows to the ranch together. So, the ranch is a location where they graze, so the total number would be all the cows. So adding 17 + 53 = 70. If that's the case, then the answer should be 70. \n\nWait, but maybe I need to make sure that there's no other interpretation. The problem says \"two more than three times the number of cows We the People has.\" So, H is 3*17 + 2. Which is 51 + 2 = 53. Then adding to We the People's cows 17 gives 70. \n\nYes, that seems straightforward. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a step I missed? Let me check again:\n\nWe the People's cows: 17. \n\nHappy Good Healthy Family: 3*(17) + 2. \n\nCalculates to 51 + 2 = 53. \n\nTotal: 17 + 53 = 70. \n\nYes, seems correct. \n\nTherefore, the number of cows in the ranch would be 70, which in a box would be \\boxed{70}.\n</think>\n\nWe start with the number of cows We the People have, which is 17. The number of cows in the Happy Good Healthy Family can be determined by expressing it as twice three times the number of cows We have, plus two. This can be written as:\n\n$$ H = 3 \\times 17 + 2 $$\n\nCalculating that:\n\n$$ H = 3 \\times 17 = 51 $$\n$$ H + 2 = 53 $$\n\nSo, the Happy Good Healthy Family has 53 cows. Adding We the People's cows to the family's cows gives:\n\n$$ 17 + 53 = 70 $$\n\nThus, the total number of cows in the ranch is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{70}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "We the People has 17 cows. Happy Good Healthy Family has two more than three times the number of cows We the People has. If their cows are taken to a ranch to graze together, how many cows will be in the ranch altogether?", "answer": "Three times the number of cows We the People has is 3*17=<<3*17=51>>51 cows.\nHappy Good Healthy Family has two more than three times the number of cows We the People has, a total of 51+2=53 cows.\nIf the cows are taken to a ranch to graze together, their total will be 53+17=<<53+17=70>>70 cows.\n#### 70", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: We the People has 17 cows. Happy Good Healthy Family has two more than three times the number of cows We the People has. If their cows are taken to a ranch to graze together, how many cows will be in the ranch altogether?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["70"], "solution": "70"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 1205, 279, 8853, 702, 220, 16, 22, 45166, 13, 23355, 7684, 43354, 12238, 702, 1378, 803, 1091, 2326, 3039, 279, 1372, 315, 45166, 1205, 279, 8853, 702, 13, 1416, 862, 45166, 525, 4429, 311, 264, 38061, 311], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 419, 3491, 3019, 553, 3019, 13, 6771, 752, 1191, 553, 8660, 1128, 279, 3491, 2727, 13, 4710, 1654, 279, 8853, 702, 220, 16, 22, 45166, 13, 5005, 11, 279, 23355, 7684, 43354, 12238, 702, 1378, 803, 1091, 2326, 3039, 279, 1372, 315, 45166, 1205, 279, 8853, 702, 13, 2055,