uble pages * 2.\n\nThen, for the 4 pages that are turned into ads, each ad takes 1/4 page. Therefore, number of ads = number of pages (from spreads) / (4 pages per ad). Wait, but if each 4 pages correspond to 4 ads, then each advertisement is 1/4 page. Therefore, total pages contributed by the sheets is:\n\nTotal pages for spreads (single + double) + pages for ads = 5 pages.\n\nBut since there are 20 single and 40 double-page spreads, that would be:\n\nNumber of pages from sheets = 20*1 + 40*2 = 20 + 80 = 100 pages.\n\nThen, the number of ads should be 4*(pages from sheets)/4 = pages from sheets. Because 4 pages sheets = 1 page ads. Therefore, number of ads is pages from sheets / (1/4) = pages from sheets * 4 pages per advertisement. Therefore, number of ads is 4 * 100 = 400 ads. Wait, that seems high.\n\nBut the problem says that for each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the presses prints a block of 4 ads. So, if 4 pages (from the sheets) are converted into 4 ads (each 0.25 pages). Therefore, total pages from the sheets is 4 pages * 4 ads = 16 pages. But each ad takes 0.25 pages. Therefore, total pages from sheets is 4 ads * 1 page ad = 4 pages. Therefore, if sheets pages is 16 pages, then 16 pages from sheets equal to 16 pages? Wait, but each ad is 0.25 pages. Therefore, 4 ads would", "source_record": {"question": "A printing press is printing brochures. The press prints 20 single-page spreads, and twice as many double-page spreads. For each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads, each of which take up a quarter of a page. The brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each. How many brochures is the printing press creating?", "answer": "There are a total of 20 single-page spreads * 2 = <<20*2=40>>40 double-page spreads.\nAs these are made up of 2 pages each, there are 40 double-page spreads * 2 pages = <<40*2=80>>80 pages in the double-page spreads.\nIn total, there are 20 single-pages + 80 double-pages = <<20+80=100>>100 pages in the spreads.\nA block of ads is printed every 4 pages, so there will be 100 pages / 4 pages/block = <<100/4=25>>25 blocks of ads.\nThis is a total of 25 blocks * 4 ads = <<25*4=100>>100 ads.\nAs each ad takes up a quarter of a page, this must create an additional 100 ads * 0.25 = <<100*0.25=25>>25 pages.\nTherefore, the press has printed 100 pages from the spreads + 25 pages of ads = <<100+25=125>>125 pages.\nSince brochures are made up of 5 pages each, this creates a total of 125 pages / 5 = <<125/5=25>>25 brochures.\n#### 25", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: A printing press is printing brochures. The press prints 20 single-page spreads, and twice as many double-page spreads. For each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads, each of which take up a quarter of a page. The brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each. How many brochures is the printing press creating?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["25"], "solution": "25"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 362, 18484, 3493, 374, 18484, 2896, 331, 1413, 13, 576, 3493, 23473, 220, 17, 15, 3175, 15112, 42553, 11, 323, 10917, 438, 1657, 1990, 15112, 42553, 13, 1752, 1817, 220, 19, 6816, 16709, 369, 279, 42553, 11], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 2896, 331, 1413, 279, 18484, 3493, 374, 6825, 13, 2055, 11, 279, 3491, 2727, 429, 279, 3493, 23473, 220, 17, 15, 3175, 15112, 42553, 323, 10917, 438, 1657, 1990, 15112, 42553, 13, 5005, 11, 369, 1817, 220, 19, 6816, 16709, 369, 279, 42553, 11, 279, 3493, 23473, 264, 2504, 315, 220, 19]}
