hich should be boxed.\n\nBut let me make sure I didn't make any mistakes in the reasoning. Maybe another way to think about it. Suppose they had 15 classes, 20 students each. Then adding 5 more classes, making 20 classes total. Each class still has 20 students. Therefore, total students would be 20*20=400. Yeah, that seems right. I don't see any errors in my steps. So the final answer is \\boxed{400}.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem at hand, follow these steps:\n\n1. Initially, Texas Integrated School has 15 classes, each with 20 students.  \n   $$\n   15 \\text{ classes} \\times 20 \\text{ students per class} = 300 \\text{ students}\n   $$\n\n2. Now, five more classes are added. This increases the total number of classes from 15 to $15 + 5 = 20$.\n\n3. Since all classes continue to be full-sized with 20 students per class, the total number of students remains $20 \\times 20 = 400$.\n\nThus, the final answer is $\\boxed{400}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Texas Integrated School has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.  They added five more classes, how many students will they have now?", "answer": "The school has 15 x 20 = <<15*20=300>>300 students right now.\nThey have an additional 5 x 20 = <<5*20=100>>100 students after adding 5 more classes.\nTherefore, Texas Integrated School will have a total of 300+100= <<300+100=400>>400 students.\n#### 400", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Texas Integrated School has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.  They added five more classes, how many students will they have now?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["400"], "solution": "400"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 8257, 49421, 6022, 702, 220, 16, 20, 6846, 323, 702, 220, 17, 15, 4143, 817, 536, 13, 220, 2379, 3694, 4236, 