2026-10-30 05:25:06 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 42, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So Jame has a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week. His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours a week. The question is asking how much more money he makes per year in his new job compared to his old job if he works 52 weeks a year.\n\nAlright, first, I need to figure out how much more his new salary is compared to his old salary each week and then multiply that by the number of weeks (52) to get the yearly difference. Let me start by calculating his new weekly earnings first.\n\nNew weekly salary: 20 dollars per hour * 40 hours per week = 800 dollars per week.\n\nOld weekly salary: 16 per hour * 25 hours per week = 400 dollars per week.\n\nSo, the difference per week between new and old is 800 - 400 = 400 dollars. Therefore, per week, he makes $400 more.\n\nNow, since he works 52 weeks a year, the total difference per year would be 52 weeks * 400 dollars/week.\n\nWait, let me check that. Is that right? Let me make sure.\n\nOld job: 400 per week.\n\nNew job: 800 per week.\n\nDifference: 400 dollars per week.\n\nOver 52 weeks, total difference would be 400 * 52.\n\nCalculating that: 400 * 52. Well, 400 * 50 is 20,000, and 400 * 2 is 800, so total 20,800. So, he makes an extra $20,800 per year.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be $\\boxed{20800}$ dollars?\n\nWait, but let me check again. Maybe I made a miscalculation here. Let's do the math step by step.\n\nOld weekly salary: 25 hours * 16 dollars/hour = 400. Correct.\n\nNew weekly salary: 40 hours * 20 dollars/hour = 800. Correct.\n\nDifference per week: 800 - 400 = 400. Correct.\n\nTotal over 52 weeks: 400 * 52.\n\n400 * 52: Let me compute 400 * 52.\n\nWell, 400*52: 400*(50+2) = 400*50 + 400*2 = 20,000 + 800 = 20,800. Yes, so it's $20,800. So, the answer is $\\boxed{20800}$.\n\nBut let me check if the problem was asking for more money than the old job. So, per year, how much more? If he makes $20,800 more per year, then that's 20,800 dollars. So, the answer is $\\boxed{20800}$. Therefore, I think that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\boxed{20800}\n</think>\n\nTo determine how much more Jame makes per year in his new job compared to his old job, we start by calculating the weekly earnings difference and then multiply that by the number of weeks he works in a year.\n\n- New weekly earnings: $ 40 \\, \\text{hours} \\times 20 \\, \\text/dollar/hour} = 800 \\, \\text{dollars} $.\n- Old weekly earnings: $ 25 \\, \\text{hours} \\times 16 \\, \\text/dollar/hour} = 400 \\, \\text{dollars} $.\n- Weekly earnings difference: $ 800 - 400 = 400 \\, \\text{dollars} $.\n- Total yearly earnings difference (for 52 weeks): $ 400 \\, \\text{dollars/week} \\times 52 \\, \\text{weeks} = 20,800 \\, \\text{dollars} $.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{20800}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?", "answer": "He makes 20*40=$<<20*40=800>>800 per week\nHe used to make 16*25=$<<16*25=400>>400 per week\nSo his raise was 800-400=$<<800-400=400>>400 per week\nSo he makes 400*52=$<<400*52=20800>>20,800 per year more\n#### 20800", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20800"], "solution": "20800"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 92088, 5221, 264, 4828, 311, 400, 17, 15, 817, 6460, 323, 4278, 220, 19, 15, 4115, 264, 2003, 13, 220, 5301, 2310, 2618, 572, 400, 16, 21, 458, 6460, 369, 220, 17, 20, 4115, 817, 2003, 13], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 2055, 92088, 702, 264, 4828, 311, 400, 17, 15, 817, 6460, 323, 4278, 220, 19, 15, 4115, 264, 2003, 13, 5301, 2310, 2618, 572, 400, 16, 21, 458, 6460, 369, 220, 17, 20, 4115, 264, 2003, 13, 576, 3405, 374, 10161, 1246, 1753, 803, 3220, 566, 3643, 817, 1042, 304, 806, 501, 2618, 7707, 311, 806]}
